# Canonical byte-stable repository representation.
#
# Every SHA-256 manifest in this project (SOURCE-HASHES.sha256,
# licenses/RELEASE-LICENSE-MANIFEST.sha256, PROJECT-METADATA.json,
# BUILD-INPUTS.lock) records the bytes of a file exactly as committed. If Git
# rewrites line endings on checkout, those hashes cannot match on another
# platform, so the maintained text files below are pinned to LF and every
# binary/payload tree is pinned to verbatim bytes.
#
# In this file the LAST matching pattern wins, so the per-extension rules come
# first and the tree-level overrides come last.

# --- Maintained text sources: canonical LF ---------------------------------
*.py        text eol=lf
*.pyi       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.spec      text eol=lf
*.sha256    text eol=lf
*.lock      text eol=lf
*.ini       text eol=lf
*.cfg       text eol=lf
*.ps1       text eol=lf

# Extensionless maintained text files (rooted so third-party copies are not
# affected; the tree overrides below still win inside vendored trees).
/LICENSE    text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Windows batch files stay CRLF. They are byte-stable either way, and CRLF is
# the safe interpreter convention, so their content is not rewritten.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# --- Binary / non-text payloads: never converted, never diffed -------------
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.pyd       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.tgz       binary
*.tar       binary
*.xz        binary
*.bz2       binary
*.7z        binary
*.whl       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.icns      binary
*.pdf       binary
*.qm        binary
*.node      binary
*.pyc       binary

# --- Verbatim trees --------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party license texts and corresponding-source trees must keep the exact
# bytes they were published/committed with. Normalizing them would modify
# third-party content and invalidate the hashed license manifest.
/licenses/**            -text
third_party_sources/**  -text

# Exception list, and it must stay the LAST word on these paths. These 27
# preserved files were committed as LF (verified byte-for-byte against the
# public main branch) while the Windows working tree held CRLF, so
# licenses/RELEASE-LICENSE-MANIFEST.sha256 recorded CRLF bytes that a clean
# checkout could never reproduce. Pinning exactly these paths to canonical LF
# gives them one checkout representation without touching the other preserved
# license files, which stay byte-verbatim under the rule above. Every other
# file in this tree — including
# /licenses/microsoft/Windows-SDK-10.0.26100.0-third-party-notices.rtf, whose
# committed blob genuinely is CRLF — is deliberately absent from this list.
/licenses/CPython-3.12.9-LICENSE.txt text eol=lf
/licenses/Node.js-22.17.0-LICENSE.txt text eol=lf
/licenses/microsoft/Visual-Studio-2022-BuildTools-Redist-local.txt text eol=lf
